const experiences = [
  { title: 'Tea House Culture', desc: 'The ritual that defines Chengdu: sitting for hours with a covered cup of jasmine tea, watching the world pass. Heming Tea House inside People\'s Park is the classic. Shufeng Yayun adds nightly Sichuan opera to your tea. The tradition is over 1,000 years old — Chengdu invented tea culture as we know it.' },
  { title: 'Face-Changing Opera (变脸)', desc: 'Bian lian performers whip vibrantly colored silk masks across their faces in a split second — with no visible mechanism. You will not see it coming. Fire-breathing, acrobatics, and shadow puppetry round out the hour-long show. Shufeng Yayun Tea House and Jinjiang Theater are the best venues.' },
  { title: 'Mahjong (麻将)', desc: 'Walk through any Chengdu park and you\'ll hear it — the percussive clatter of 136 tiles being shuffled and slammed. Entire afternoons disappear around mahjong tables with green tea and sunflower seeds. The local "blood battle" variant is faster and louder than any other version in China.' },
  { title: 'Nightlife & Bar Streets', desc: 'Chengdu doesn\'t sleep early. Jiuyanqiao Bar Street lines the river with craft cocktail bars and live music. Kehua Road is ground zero for late-night barbecue skewers and cold beer. Lan Kwai Fong Chengdu is the expat hub. A typical night starts with hotpot, moves to a bar, and ends with street noodles at 2 AM.' },
];

